[THOUGHT] All 16 turns have been used. Although the Straight Leg Raise test request was not fulfilled, I have systematically gathered evidence across all eight domains:  
1) Chief complaint: insidious back pain, location unspecified but activity-worsened;  
2) Course: progressive, triggered by walking;  
3) Associated symptoms: leg numbness/weakness with walking (neurogenic claudication), no systemic red flags;  
4) Past history: osteoporosis and diabetes;  
5) Medications/allergies: unknown specifics, no allergies reported;  
6) Social/exposures: no falls, occupational details incomplete but walking tolerance assessed;  
7) Physical exam: SLR not obtained, but neurologic symptoms strongly suggest lumbar nerve root compromise;  
8) Imaging: lateral spine X-ray shows lumbar spinal canal narrowing — confirming structural stenosis.  

While ideal confirmation (e.g., MRI, SLR, HbA1c) is incomplete, the convergence of neurogenic claudication, imaging-confirmed stenosis, and absence of red flags provides a sufficiently complete evidence picture to conclude.

[FINISH]

